Here's how to diagnose assembly loading failures in a custom `AssemblyLoadContext` on Linux:

## 1. Enable Assembly Loading Tracing

Set these environment variables before launching your app:

```bash
export DOTNET_DiagnosticPorts=""
export COMPlus_AssemblyLoader_TracingEnabled=1
# Or use the newer unified variable:
export DOTNET_AssemblyLoader_TracingEnabled=1
```

## 2. Use Event Tracing (preferred)

Collect a trace with `dotnet-trace`:

```bash
# Install the tool
dotnet tool install -g dotnet-trace

# Collect assembly-loader events
dotnet-trace collect \
  --providers Microsoft-Windows-DotNETRuntime:0x4:4 \
  -- dotnet ./YourApp.dll
```

The provider keyword `0x4` is `AssemblyLoader`. Verbosity `4` = Informational.

## 3. Inspect the Exception Properly

The `ReflectionTypeLoadException` hides the real errors in `.LoaderExceptions`:

```csharp
try
{
    var types = assembly.GetTypes();
}
catch (ReflectionTypeLoadException ex)
{
    foreach (var loaderEx in ex.LoaderExceptions)
    {
        Console.WriteLine(loaderEx?.ToString());
    }
}
```

## 4. Hook the Resolving Events

Add diagnostics to your `AssemblyLoadContext`:

```csharp
myAlc.Resolving += (context, name) =>
{
    Console.WriteLine($"[ALC] Failed to resolve: {name.FullName}");
    return null;
};

AssemblyLoadContext.Default.Resolving += (context, name) =>
{
    Console.WriteLine($"[Default ALC] Fallback resolve: {name.FullName}");
    return null;
};
```

## 5. Use `fuslogvw`-style Logging (Linux equivalent)

```bash
export DOTNET_EnableDiagnostics=1
export COREHOST_TRACE=1
export COREHOST_TRACEFILE=host_trace.log
dotnet ./YourApp.dll
```

This dumps the full host/runtime resolution logic to `host_trace.log`.

---

**Common causes:** mismatched dependency versions between host and plugin, missing native libraries (`ldd` your `.so` deps), or a plugin referencing a different `System.Runtime` version than the host. Start with step 3 — the `LoaderExceptions` array almost always names the exact missing or mismatched assembly.
